age let's check our  time here and the next step down here  then is to get rid of a little bit of  that filtering that I did on the image  collection so if you remember earlier I  filtered it down to a particular month  and then pick the first image in there  so I filtered it down to one image but  in this case I'm gonna run a query that  instead of doing one day let's expand it  out to like a week maybe and once you  get back that collection I can run a  aggregation on it like take the median  value of the images if they're  overlapping because these satellite  paths do overlap especially if you  expand out in time let me run that and  as I move back up here you'll start  seeing you know how many images were  collected for a particular week and the  nice thing about this is I can you know  start panning around to wherever I want  in the world and the only time the data  is being requested is that when it comes  in to the viewpoint so it's loading a  really small subset of that potential  you know multi terabytes to petabyte of  data in there probably you know for that  small time period it's not going to be  anywhere near petabyte but if you are  feeling very adventurous then you could  say something like hey let's go back and  do a full year's worth of data here  approximately a year let's do that run  it again here and now you're gonna start  seeing you know take a little bit longer  because in any point in on earth it's  going to be around 25 images  in a year for this particular sensor but  it can gather all those together and  start aggregating them as I said before  the aggregation that we're doing right  now for that stack is called a median  composite so it's like ranking all the  values and then just taking the median  value but we also have other ones like  min and Max mean they actually just do  the point of reference here we're gonna  find a go to Texas where it's sort of  cloudy let it load up here again a  little bit and then I'm going to change  the 