ing is  just in the visible bands red green and  blue did a little bit with the  near-infrared  but these satellites do collect a lot  more information Landsat itself has two  bands in the shortwave infrared there's  a thermal band there there's one that's  just sensitive to like high clouds and  some of the other sensors like modis or  some of the ones from the European Space  Agency they might have 30 or more bands  that are sensitive to different parts of  the electromagnetic spectrum it totally  depends on what you're interested in  monitoring which bands you're going to  pick the ones that are really really  useful though are the thermal bands  which tell you a little bit about the  temperature of the earth because I can  give you an idea of the energy balance I  do a lot of work with people that are  trying to monitor water use by  agricultural crops and getting an idea  of the thermal signature of those fields  is really important for us mating and  how much water is being used  so the question is whether earth engine  has access to high-resolution imagery  from a technical standpoint yes from a  licensing standpoint not usually there  is some data from USDA FSA it's called  nape data which is that one meter  resolution that's available for the  United States on like a three-year  rotating cycle internally we use it for  some of our data that we collect but  that's not necessarily public data let  me think  but the other data providers are putting  out like DMS des live from right lidar  which might be half a meter or  resolution or so so it's totally  dependent on the data source not really  earth engine and so it's basically what  you can already get available publicly  at high resolution we can get into Earth  engine I didn't point out when I was  talking there but there is a way to  upload your own imagery too if you don't  see it in Earth engine and you have a  license to your own high-resolution  commercial imagery you can upload it and  work with it yo